Sure! Let's implement a secure login flow in your web application using **ASP.NET Core MVC** or **Minimal APIs**, depending on your structure.

You want:

1. The user to be **redirected to `login.html`** if not authenticated.
2. On correct credentials from `appsettings.json`, redirect to `purchaseorderview.html`.
3. Prevent bypassing the login by directly accessing `purchaseorderview.html`.

---

### ✅ **Before We Start** – Required Files:

We will work with:

- `Program.cs`
- `appsettings.json` – contains admin credentials
- `LoginController.cs`
- `login.html`
- `purchaseorderview.html`
- Configure authentication (either cookie or session-based)

---

### 📂 Step-by-step Plan

#### Step 1: ✅ Update your `appsettings.json`:

```json
{
  "AdminCredentials": {
    "Username": "admin",
    "Password": "admin123"
  }
}
```

---

#### Step 2: ✅ `Program.cs` – Add Middleware and Services

Now, please paste your `Program.cs`, but meanwhile, here’s a typical setup using **cookie authentication** style:

```csharp
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication.Cookies;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Configuration;
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;

var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

// Load configuration (Admin credentials from appsettings.json)
builder.Configuration.AddJsonFile("appsettings.json", optional: false, reloadOnChange: true);

// Add services
builder.Services.AddRazorPages();
builder.Services.AddControllersWithViews();
builder.Services.AddAuthentication(CookieAuthenticationDefaults.AuthenticationScheme)
    .AddCookie(options =>
    {
        options.LoginPath = "/login.html";
        options.AccessDeniedPath = "/login.html";
    });
builder.Services.AddAuthorization();

var app = builder.Build();

// Middlewares
app.UseStaticFiles();
app.UseRouting();
app.UseAuthentication();
app.UseAuthorization();

// Map controllers
app.MapControllers();
app.MapFallbackToFile("login.html");

app.Run();
```

> 📌 This uses cookie-based authentication.

---

#### Step 3: ✅ Create `LoginController.cs`

```csharp
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Configuration;
using System.Security.Claims;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication.Cookies;

[Route("[controller]")]
public class LoginController : Controller
{
    private readonly IConfiguration _config;

    public LoginController(IConfiguration config)
    {
        _config = config;
    }

    [HttpPost("authenticate")]
    public async Task<IActionResult> Authenticate([FromForm] string username, [FromForm] string password)
    {
        var adminUsername = _config["AdminCredentials:Username"];
        var adminPassword = _config["AdminCredentials:Password"];

        if (username == adminUsername && password == adminPassword)
        {
            var claims = new List<Claim>
            {
                new Claim(ClaimTypes.Name, username)
            };

            var claimsIdentity = new ClaimsIdentity(claims, CookieAuthenticationDefaults.AuthenticationScheme);
            var authProperties = new AuthenticationProperties
            {
                IsPersistent = true
            };

            await HttpContext.SignInAsync(CookieAuthenticationDefaults.AuthenticationScheme,
                new ClaimsPrincipal(claimsIdentity),
                authProperties);

            return Redirect("/purchaseorderview.html");
        }

        // Incorrect credentials - redirect back to login
        return Redirect("/login.html?error=invalid");
    }

    [HttpPost("logout")]
    public async Task<IActionResult> Logout()
    {
        await HttpContext.SignOutAsync(CookieAuthenticationDefaults.AuthenticationScheme);
        return Redirect("/login.html");
    }
}
```

---

#### Step 4: ✅ Create `login.html`

Make a simple form that posts to the login controller:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Login</title>
</head>
<body>
    <form method="post" action="/Login/authenticate">
        <label>Username:</label>
        <input name="username" type="text" required /><br />
        <label>Password:</label>
        <input name="password" type="password" required /><br />
        <button type="submit">Login</button>
    </form>
</body>
</html>
```

---

#### Step 5: ✅ Secure `purchaseorderview.html`

You have 2 ways to protect static HTML from unauthenticated access.

##### 🔒 Option 1: Serve it via a Razor Page or MVC View with `[Authorize]`:

1. Move `purchaseorderview.html` to a Razor Page:
   - Rename `purchaseorderview.html` to `purchaseorderview.cshtml`.
   - In the `Pages` folder (`/Pages/PurchaseOrderView.cshtml`), add:

```html
@page
@attribute [Authorize]
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Purchase Order</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Welcome Admin to Purchase Order View</h1>
</body>
</html>
```

2. Update `Program.cs` to support Razor Pages:

```csharp
app.MapRazorPages(); // Add this!
```

Now if anyone is not logged in, `/PurchaseOrderView` will be redirected to login.

---

##### 🧱 Option 2: Return HTML from a secure controller method

> Static files can't be protected directly, unless you serve them via a controller + `[Authorize]`.

Example:

```csharp
[Authorize]
[Route("purchaseorderview")]
public class PurchaseOrderController : Controller
{
    [HttpGet]
    public IActionResult Index()
    {
        return PhysicalFile(Path.Combine(Directory.GetCurrentDirectory(), "wwwroot/purchaseorderview.html"), "text/html");
    }
}
```

Access via `/purchaseorderview`, and it will load the static HTML only if logged in.

---

#### ✅ Final Notes

- Make sure both `login.html` and `purchaseorderview.html` are placed under `wwwroot/`.
- Test the login → redirection → try accessing protected page directly.

---

### ✅ Summary

- User accesses root → redirected to `/login.html`.
- After login (credentials from `appsettings.json`) → redirected to `/purchaseorderview.html`.
- Unauthorized access to `/purchaseorderview.html` → redirected to `/login.html`.

---

